WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
MCP systems move data through prompts, user files, retrieved resources, tool results,

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.600
server metadata, logs, cached content, memory features, and downstream services.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.280
Each movement changes exposure.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.325
A file that was safe in a private workspace may become riskier if placed into a model

00:00:15.325 --> 00:00:15.840
context,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.560
copied into logs, cached for later use, or sent to a remote server.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.060
Data handling rules should describe what can enter the system, where it can be stored,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.350
and how long it can remain.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.520
Trust boundaries are points where data, identity, permissions, or assumptions change.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.760
User input crosses a boundary when it influences model behavior.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.750
External content crosses a boundary when it enters context.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.520
Tool results cross a boundary when they move from a downstream system back to the host.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.550
Server metadata crosses a boundary when it influences model tool selection.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.570
Data flow diagrams should show these transitions instead of treating the AI application as

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.480
one opaque box.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.495
External content should be treated as untrusted input.

00:00:50.495 --> 00:00:55.445
A document, web page, issue, email, repository file, or resource may contain inaccurate,

00:00:55.445 --> 00:00:56.870
stale, or misleading text.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.470
That content can still be useful,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.400
but it should not override trusted configuration or policy.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.740
Provenance helps the system and user understand where content came from.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.650
Tenant isolation and per-user filtering prevent one user's data from becoming another

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:09.520
user's context.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.020
Data minimization reduces how much sensitive information is exposed.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.430
Retention and logging require careful design.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:19.795
Logs can support investigation, but they should avoid unnecessary secrets, credentials,

00:01:19.795 --> 00:01:22.209
regulated data, or sensitive user content.

00:01:22.209 --> 00:01:24.550
Cached content and memory can improve usability,

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.750
but they need boundaries and deletion rules.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.270
Sensitive data should be protected before it reaches the model where possible.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.260
MCP fundamentals are not only about connecting systems;

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.560
they are about keeping information understandable and controlled as it moves across

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.760
systems.
